Got it — let’s walk through how you can capture and analyze a TLS handshake in Wireshark in a clear, step-by-step way. I’ll keep it practical so you can follow along without getting lost.

1. Prepare Wireshark for Capture

Open Wireshark and select the network interface you use for internet access (e.g., Wi-Fi or Ethernet).
In the Capture Filter field, you can limit traffic to TLS/SSL by using:tcp port 443

This ensures you only capture HTTPS traffic.


2. Initiate a TLS Handshake

Open a browser and visit any HTTPS website (preferably one you haven’t visited recently to avoid cached sessions).
This will trigger a fresh TLS handshake between your client and the server.


3. Stop the Capture

Once the page loads, stop the capture in Wireshark to avoid unnecessary data clutter.


4. Filter for TLS Handshake Packets

In the Display Filter bar, type:tls.handshake

This will show only handshake-related packets.


5. Analyze the TLS Handshake Steps
Typical sequence you’ll see:

Client Hello – Client proposes TLS version, cipher suites, and extensions.
Server Hello – Server selects TLS version, cipher suite, and sends its certificate.
Certificate – Server’s public key and identity details.
Server Key Exchange (if needed) – Parameters for key agreement.
Client Key Exchange – Client sends key material (encrypted).
Change Cipher Spec – Both sides signal they’re switching to encrypted communication.
Finished – Both confirm handshake completion.


6. Inspect Details

Click on a Client Hello packet → expand Transport Layer Security → Handshake Protocol to see:

Supported cipher suites
TLS extensions (e.g., SNI, ALPN)


Click on Server Hello to see the chosen cipher suite and TLS version.
Check the Certificate packet to view the server’s certificate chain.


